David Ernst

Usage:
    plex-letterboxd-sync [options] <export.zip|account>
Argument:
    <export.zip|account>
                        A Letterboxd data export (Settings > Data > Export),
                        which holds everything the account has logged - or the
                        account's name, whose public RSS feed holds only the
                        fifty or so most recent diary entries.
Environment:
    mediaServer         The server: plex (the default) or jellyfin.
    plexUrl             The Plex server, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:32400
    plexToken           Its X-Plex-Token.
    jellyfinUrl         The Jellyfin server, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:8096
    jellyfinApiKey      An API key it made (Dashboard > API Keys).
    jellyfinUser        The user whose films these are.
Options:
    -s, --sections <names>
                    Only the server's movie libraries with these titles,
                    comma-separated. Default: every movie library.
    -l, --to-plex-only
                    Letterboxd -> server only: mark films played and rated, and
                    write no CSV.
    -o, --output <file>
                    Write the CSV for Letterboxd's importer to <file>. Default:
                    logs/letterboxd-import-<date>.csv in the medialib data
                    directory.
    -S, --state <file>
                    Remember what Letterboxd has, and what was sent to it, in
                    <file>, and keep a copy of the server's library beside it,
                    so the next run reads only what changed. Default:
                    data/<server>-letterboxd-state.json in the medialib data
                    directory, e.g. data/plex-letterboxd-state.json.
    -a, --account <id>
                    Read the plays of this account rather than the default one:
                    on Plex its number on the server, rather than its owner's
                    (1) - the numbers are listed at <plexUrl>/accounts; on
                    Jellyfin a user's name, rather than jellyfinUser.
    -f, --full      Read the whole library rather than what changed since the
                    last run - which a run also does by itself once a week, to
                    see films removed or marked unplayed.
    -p, --preview   Preview: say what would be marked, rated, added and
                    exported, and change nothing - not the server, not the
                    CSVs, not the state file. The copy of the library is kept.
    -h, --help      Print this help page.
    -A, --ai        Let language models help (docs/models.md). Without -A no
                    step asks one; the commands this one starts are handed it.
    -v, --verbose   Also say why each item was handled the way it was, and on a
                    failure print the tool's whole output rather than its last
                    lines.
    -q, --quiet     Print errors only - no progress and no warnings.
    -x, --set <name=value>
                    Override a setting for this run, over the settings file;
                    repeatable. A secret is refused - the settings file is where
                    it goes. The settings page lists the names.
